// Lesson 1.4 — Movement I: Input, Direction, Vector
// First-introduction lesson: FULL scaffold (walkthrough level 1).
//
// The old version of this lesson handed students three one-line snippets from
// foreign machines (forklift / elevator / conveyor) and a callout of thirteen
// questions, then expected them to assemble movement from that. The forklift
// snippet in particular — `float steeringIntent = Input.GetAxis("Horizontal");`
// — taught a declare-and-initialize shape that is the WRONG shape here, with
// nothing said about why. Students mirrored it, declared their input variable
// inside Update(), and it re-initialized every frame. That section is gone; the
// walkthrough replaces it and 1.3's scope rule is re-invoked at the exact line
// where it matters.
//
// STRUCTURE: the clicks are "In the Editor" (open the file, read the component,
// the Input Manager, play, tune the speed knob, commit) and the file work is
// "In the Code" (the input-method table, the socratic, the walkthrough, the bug
// hunt). The old "Read the Docs" label is absorbed into those two: its table
// stays as the lookup, and its virtual-axis paragraph became the Input Manager
// editor step, which is where it is actually read.
//
// SECOND PASS (words, not lines — "a concept gets explained at most twice"):
// the declare-vs-assign point was being made in four sections (Break It Down
// part 1, the walkthrough's field note, the walkthrough's shadowing note, the
// bug hunt). It is now made once, at the assignment line in the walkthrough,
// and the bug hunt only breaks it. Culled by name: the walkthrough's
// "Why here and not inside Update()" note (the assignment line's note says the
// same thing), the "In the next lesson you will compare..." sentence in the trap
// callout (pre-explained 1.5), the "course design" sentence in the
// starting-state callout, and the walkthrough's note on where the Vector2 is
// declared. Every annotation was rewritten to one or two sentences.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import { renderWalkthrough, renderAskBanner } from '../../lib/walkthrough';
import { renderDecompose } from '../../lib/decompose';

// The two recurring questions, applied to movement. Level 1, so every part
// is given with what it does AND where to read about it — the student has
// never split a problem before, so the splitting is demonstrated, not asked
// for. The parts match the walkthrough's four steps: the split is not a
// warm-up that gets thrown away, it IS the plan the build follows.
const DECOMPOSE = {
  big:
    'Move the character when I press the arrow keys. As one problem, that is far too big to ' +
    'start typing.',
  parts: [
    {
      name: 'Find out which way the player is pushing',
      does: 'Ask the keyboard for two numbers, one per axis, each between -1 and 1, and keep them in fields so they survive the frame.',
      doc: 'The axis reads in the quick reference, and 1.3\'s declare-vs-assign rule.',
      href: '../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Turn those two numbers into one direction',
      does: 'A pair of numbers is awkward to move by. Combine them into one `Vector2`: a single value meaning "this far, in this direction".',
      doc: '`Vector2` in the quick reference.',
      href: '../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Scale that direction by speed and by the frame',
      does: 'Multiply by `moveSpeed` to turn a direction into a distance, and by `Time.deltaTime` so the distance is per second rather than per frame.',
      doc: 'The `deltaTime` row in the quick reference.',
      href: '../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Move the player by that distance, every frame',
      does: 'Hand the finished offset to the Player\'s own Transform, which adds it to where the Player already is.',
      doc: '`transform.Translate(v)` in the quick reference.',
      href: '../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html',
    },
  ],
  why:
    'Four parts, and only one is the part you would have thought of first. "Move the player" ' +
    'sounds like one action; it is a read, a build, a scale and an offset.',
  sizeTest:
    'The test for a finished split: could you look this part up on its own, in one search? ' +
    '"Move the player" cannot; "get keyboard input as a number" can.',
  turns: [
    'Read the input, build a direction, apply the movement. Which of those three would still ' +
      'work unchanged if you swapped the keyboard for a gamepad stick — and why is that a sign ' +
      'the split is a good one?',
  ],
};

const WALK = {
  ask: 'how do I move an object in Unity?',
  file: 'Assets/Scripts/PlayerController.cs',
  label: 'In the Code',
  intro: 'Type these into the class body, in this order.',
  level: 1,
  steps: [
    {
      title: 'Open the class and add the speed knob',
      lead: 'PlayerController is already on the Player with two empty methods. It needs one number first: how fast to move.',
      lines: [
        { code: '// PlayerController.cs — the whole file. Add the field inside the class.', what: '' },
        { code: 'using UnityEngine;', what: '' },
        { code: '', what: '' },
        { code: 'public class PlayerController : MonoBehaviour', what: '' },
        { code: '{', what: '' },
        { code: '    [SerializeField] private float moveSpeed = 5f;', what: 'Units travelled per second. `[SerializeField]` puts it in the Inspector, so it can be tuned while the game runs.' },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: 'Declare the fields that remember the input',
      lead: 'These two go in the class body, next to the speed knob.',
      lines: [
        { code: '// PlayerController.cs — still the class body, below the speed knob.', what: '' },
        { code: '    private float inputX;', what: 'Left/right as a number: `-1`, `0` or `1`. In the class body it belongs to the Player, not to the frame.' },
        { code: '    private float inputY;', what: 'The same for up/down: `-1`, `0`, or `1`.' },
        { code: '', what: '' },
        { code: '    void Update()', what: 'The method Unity calls every frame.' },
        { code: '    {', what: '' },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: 'Read the keyboard',
      lead: 'Ask Unity what is being pressed right now, and store it in the two fields you just declared.',
      lines: [
        { code: '        inputX = Input.GetAxis("Horizontal");', what: 'Reads the virtual axis `"Horizontal"` into `inputX`. There is **no `float` in front of it** — the variable exists, so this line assigns.', why: 'Write `float inputX = ...` here and you shadow the field with a temporary variable that dies with the frame. Most students hit this bug.' },
        { code: '        inputY = Input.GetAxis("Vertical");', what: 'The same read for up/down. Spell it exactly `"Vertical"`: capitals matter.' },
        { code: '', what: '' },
        { code: '        Vector2 movement = new Vector2(', what: 'Builds the direction. This one **is** declared here: it is used two lines later, and nothing next frame needs it.' },
        { code: '            inputX * moveSpeed * Time.deltaTime,', what: 'Horizontal distance this frame: direction × speed × frame time.' },
        { code: '            inputY * moveSpeed * Time.deltaTime);', what: 'The same for vertical. The closing `);` finishes the `Vector2`.' },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: 'Apply the movement',
      lead: 'You hold a displacement — "move this far from where I am." Hand it to the Transform.',
      lines: [
        { code: '        transform.Translate(movement);', what: 'Offsets the Player\'s position by that displacement. Every GameObject already has a `transform`.' },
        { code: '    }', what: '' },
      ],
    },
  ],
  // The reference is deliberately NOT the same shape as the walkthrough above
  // it. Both are correct; this one works out the distance-per-frame once and
  // multiplies the whole direction by it, where the walkthrough scaled each
  // component. So the file says on its own terms whether it was built or
  // pasted — and a student comparing the two has to read for meaning rather
  // than diff for text, which is the point. What must NOT change is anything a
  // later lesson builds on: inputX, inputY, moveSpeed and the Vector2 all stay,
  // because 1.5 is about that vector's length and 1.6 reads those two inputs.
  finalFile: `using UnityEngine;

public class PlayerController : MonoBehaviour
{
    [SerializeField] private float moveSpeed = 5f;

    private float inputX;
    private float inputY;

    void Update()
    {
        inputX = Input.GetAxis("Horizontal");
        inputY = Input.GetAxis("Vertical");

        float stepThisFrame = moveSpeed * Time.deltaTime;
        Vector2 movement = new Vector2(inputX, inputY) * stepThisFrame;

        transform.Translate(movement);
    }
}`,
  finalNote:
    'Compare it against your own file: `inputX` and `inputY` declared in the class body and only assigned inside `Update()`, and every displacement line scaled by the frame time.',
};

const body = `<h1>1.4 — Movement I: Input, Direction, Vector</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">Movement looks effortless in a game. In code it is an algorithm: <strong>read the player's input → turn it into a distance → move the character</strong>. Every line is given below.</p>

<p><strong>Starting point:</strong> <code>Assets/Scripts/PlayerController.cs</code> is a shell — two empty methods. The Player has a Transform and nothing else: no physics, no collider.</p>

${renderAskBanner('how do I move an object in Unity?', 1)}

${renderDecompose('1.4', DECOMPOSE, 1)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header"><span class="activity-label">In the Editor</span><span class="activity-time">~12 min</span></div>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Project window →</strong> <code>Assets/Scripts</code>, double-click <code>PlayerController.cs</code> to open it.</li>
        <li><strong>Hierarchy →</strong> select the <strong>Player</strong>, then <strong>Inspector →</strong> read the <strong>Player Controller (Script)</strong> component: two methods, nothing to tune yet.</li>
        <li><strong>Game view →</strong> press <strong>Play</strong> and hold WASD. Nothing moves — the shell runs and does nothing.</li>
        <li><strong>Edit → Project Settings → Input Manager:</strong> read the <strong>Horizontal</strong> and <strong>Vertical</strong> axes. Neither is a single key, and a gamepad stick is already in the list.</li>
        <li><strong>Game view →</strong> press <strong>Play</strong> once your script is written and hold WASD. The character glides in all four directions.</li>
        <li><strong>Inspector →</strong> while the game runs, change the <strong>Move Speed</strong> box. Then <strong>GitHub Desktop →</strong> commit and push your movement code.</li>
    </ol>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header"><span class="activity-label">In the Code</span><span class="activity-time">~20 min</span></div>
    <p>Four ways to ask Unity for movement. You write the first and the last today; the middle two are here because choosing between them is a design decision.</p>
    <table>
        <tr><th>Unity API</th><th>Returns</th><th>Behaviour</th></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Input.GetAxis("Horizontal")</code></td><td><code>float</code> (−1.0 to 1.0)</td><td><strong>Smooth ramp.</strong> Accelerates to −1 or +1 and coasts back to 0, so movement has a little weight.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Input.GetAxisRaw("Horizontal")</code></td><td><code>float</code> (−1, 0 or 1)</td><td><strong>Instant snap.</strong> Exactly −1, 0 or 1 the moment a key changes, so it stops on a dime.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Input.GetKey(KeyCode.D)</code></td><td><code>bool</code></td><td><strong>Binary state.</strong> True while the physical D key is held — that key only.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>transform.Translate(vector)</code></td><td><code>void</code></td><td><strong>Offsets the position by a vector</strong> — a <em>relative</em> step: "move this far from where I am."</td></tr>
    </table>
    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Evaluating Multiple Ways to Solve a Problem</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>A driving game wants weight and momentum: <code>Input.GetAxis("Horizontal")</code> or <code>Input.GetAxisRaw("Horizontal")</code>? Why?</li>
            <li>A grid puzzle where you must stop on a dime: why would <code>Input.GetAxisRaw("Horizontal")</code> be the better choice?</li>
            <li>A player wants the arrow keys instead of WASD. Why is the axis <code>"Horizontal"</code> more flexible than <code>Input.GetKey(KeyCode.D)</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
${renderWalkthrough(WALK)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header"><span class="activity-label">Bug Hunt</span><span class="activity-time">~20 min</span></div>
    <p>Your character glides in all four directions. Now prove you know why.</p>
    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Testing Assumptions</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Delete the per-frame multiplier from both displacement lines. Tap a direction key: why does the character cross the whole map in one frame?</li>
            <li>Set the <strong>Move Speed</strong> box to <code>0</code> while the game runs, then to <code>30</code>. Describe the difference.</li>
            <li>Swap the two numbers you pass into the direction — vertical where the horizontal belongs. What does the character do now, and why?</li>
            <li>Move the declaration: cut <code>private float inputX;</code> from the class body and declare <code>float inputX</code> inside <code>Update()</code>, on the line where the read happens. Does the character still move? Explain with <em>declare</em> and <em>assign</em>.</li>
            <li>Walk your character into a wall and keep pushing. What happens? Look back at the component list from <a href="1.2.html">1.2</a>: what is the Player <em>missing</em>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>Keep that last answer.</strong> Walking through walls is not a mistake in your code: <code>transform.Translate(vector)</code> edits coordinates directly, so nothing ever told it a wall was there.
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="checklist">
    <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>State the sub-problems of movement: input → vector → distance → translation</li>
        <li><strong>Point at your own file and say which lines declare and which lines assign</strong></li>
        <li>Choose between a smooth axis, a raw axis and a single key for a given game</li>
        <li>Explain why movement has to be scaled by how long the frame took</li>
        <li>Tune the speed knob while the game runs</li>
        <li>Explain why your character can walk through a wall</li>
    </ul>
</div>

